      "left": "a heavier, misplaced load discovered after the fact",
      "right": "what many failure investigations reduce to"
    }
  ],
  "why": "Every load case draws its own diagram, and the design must survive the envelope of all of them."
};

const ceFloorPlace = {
  "prompt": "Pedestrians walk at 1.6–2.4 steps per second, with harmonics at two and three times that. Slide the marker to the frequency guidance pushes rhythmic-occupancy floors above.",
  "labels": [
    "0 Hz",
    "3 Hz",
    "6 Hz",
    "9 Hz",
    "12 Hz"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 12
  },
  "answer": 7.5,
  "tolerance": 1,
  "why": "About 7.5 Hz — not so the floor is stronger, but so the crowd cannot find their rhythm. The worked floor sits at ≈6.4 Hz: above a brisk walk's second harmonic, but not comfortably."
};

const ceRegimesCompare = {
  "prompt": "The same three equations, with and without inertia — drag the divider:",
  "before": {
    "label": "statics",
    "text": "Bodies never accelerate: zero net force and zero net moment, three statements per body — a parked truck loads the deck no harder than it weighs."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "dynamics",
    "text": "Add mass × acceleration: rolling trucks arrest suspension and hit harder (amplification often ~1.3), and rhythmic loads pump energy at natural frequencies."
  },
  "why": "Same three equations, new inertia terms — and suddenly crowds can walk a deck into swaying."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/civil-engineering/foundational/statics-and-dynamics-for-civil-engineers">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Civil Engineering, and it asks for almost nothing from
    earlier study — only everyday arithmetic, a little trigonometry (what sine and cosine mean
    for a right triangle), and a willingness to draw pictures before calculating. Everything
    else is built here, step by step.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>state what engineers mean by <strong>equilibrium</strong>, and write down the three equations that express it in two dimensions;</li>
    <li>draw a free-body diagram — the subject's signature move — and explain why it always comes first;</li>
    <li>work out the forces in a simple truss, member by member, and say which bars pull and which push;</li>
    <li>explain what a cross-section's centroid and second moment of area measure, and why the same plank is four times stiffer one way up than the other;</li>
    <li>read a bending moment diagram and point to the section that must be strongest;</li>
    <li>distinguish kinematics (describing motion) from kinetics (explaining it), and explain why a crowd can set a bridge swaying even when its strength is never in doubt.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit governs the whole subject: forces are traced, not assumed. Every newton that
    enters a structure has a path to the ground, and the engineer's job is to follow it.
  </p>

  <h2>The engineer's promise: proving a bridge before it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Civil engineers make an unusual promise: this structure will hold, for decades, through
    storms, crowds, traffic and earthquakes — and we can prove it on paper before a single
    bucket of concrete is poured. The proof rests on a handful of ideas that fit in one
    lesson. <strong>Statics</strong> is the branch of mechanics that studies bodies at rest or
    moving at constant velocity; when loads accelerate, statics grows into
    <strong>dynamics</strong>. Together they are the grammar in which every bridge, tower and
    floor is justified.
  </p>
  <p>
    The everyday picture is a set of scales. A bridge deck under traffic is a giant balance:
    the loads push down, the supports push up, and the two must match exactly — or the bridge
    accelerates, which for a successful bridge means never. What makes the subject rich is
    that matching the totals is only the first question. A structure can have its forces
    perfectly balanced and still fail, because one hidden member is quietly carrying far more
    than its share. The engineer's task is to trace where every newton flows: down through the
    deck, along the beams, into the columns, and finally into the ground. The tool for tracing
    is the <strong>free-body diagram</strong>: isolate the piece you care about, cut it free
    from the world, and replace everything you cut away with the forces it was exerting. An
    abstract question — is this truss safe? — becomes a concrete one: here are the forces,
    here are the equations, solve.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from free-body diagram to internal forces" widget="equationStep" config={staticsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the five steps are one story told in stages, not five
    separate topics. A free-body diagram feeds equilibrium; equilibrium either finishes the
    job (determinate) or admits it needs material properties (indeterminate); cutting the body
    turns outside forces into inside forces; and when the body accelerates, the same three
    equations gain inertia terms and become dynamics. Keep the whole ladder in view — every
    later section stands on exactly one of these rungs.
  </p>

  <h2>Equilibrium: three equations that hold the world still</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Equilibrium</strong> is the central concept of statics. A body is in equilibrium
    when two things are simultaneously true: the net force on it is zero <em>and</em> the net
    turning effect of those forces is zero. This is Newton's first law in engineering dress —
    an object at rest stays at rest unless a net force acts. "Net" is the operative word:
    forces cancel only when every direction is accounted for.
  </p>
  <p>
    The turning effect has its own name: the <strong>moment</strong> (physicists often say
    <strong>torque</strong>). Push a door near its hinges and it barely swings; push the edge
    and it flies open. The moment of a force is the force multiplied by its <strong>lever
    arm</strong> — the perpendicular distance from the pivot to the line of the force. The
    see-saw is the classic demonstration: a heavy child on one side balances a light child on
    the other only if the light one sits farther out, because distance multiplies force. For a
    point mass at distance r from the pivot, the turning power of a force F is
    <code>M = r·F</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    For any one body in two dimensions, equilibrium is exactly three equations: the forces sum
    to zero horizontally, the forces sum to zero vertically, and the moments sum to zero about
    any point you choose. Three equations, no more — and that budget is the subject's hard
    currency. If a structure has three unknown support forces, statics alone finds them all
    and the structure is called <strong>statically determinate</strong>. If it has more
    unknowns than equations — a continuous beam over several supports, a rigid frame —
    equilibrium cannot finish the job, and material stiffness must be invited in. That is
    <strong>indeterminate</strong> analysis, and it waits for a later lesson.
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="run the budget check — can statics finish alone?" widget="branch" config={ceDetermBranch} />
  <p>
    One idealisation powers all of this: the <strong>rigid body</strong>. Statics treats every
    body as perfectly undeformable — geometry never changes, so forces never redistribute.
    Real structures do flex, and the next lesson studies exactly that gap; within its limits,
    though, the rigid-body model is how full-scale bridges are checked.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "if the vertical forces balance, the structure is fine."
  It seems reasonable — gravity pulls down, so balancing upward forces feels like the whole
  story. But forces can cancel while turning effects do not. Two equal, opposite forces
  applied on either side of a steering wheel produce zero net force and still spin the wheel
  furiously. A structure needs ΣM = 0 as well as ΣF = 0, which is precisely why a crane's
  counterweight is placed where it is: not to add force, but to cancel moments.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — the balance trap" widget="judge" config={ceMomentJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> statics rests on one claim — a body at rest has zero net force
    <em>and</em> zero net moment — which becomes three equations per body in two dimensions.
    Everything an engineer subsequently does is a way of feeding those equations.
  </p>

  <h2>Trusses and frames: equilibrium made visible</h2>
  <p>
    A truss is a skeleton of straight bars joined at their ends into triangles, and its genius
    is a single assumption: the joints behave like pins, so each bar carries force only along
    its own axis. A bar can pull (<strong>tension</strong>) or push
    (<strong>compression</strong>), but it cannot bend. Such bars — loaded only at their two
    ends — are called <strong>two-force members</strong>, and the label is a gift: at every
    joint, each one contributes a force of known direction. That turns a daunting tangle of
    steel into pure arithmetic, because each joint is a small body in equilibrium.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="file each member by what it does" widget="sort-buckets" config={ceTrussSort} />
  <p>
    The <strong>method of joints</strong> exploits this one pin at a time. Take two rafters
    meeting at an apex, joined by a horizontal tie, with a 10 kN load hanging at the apex. At
    the apex there are two unknown bar forces and exactly two useful equilibrium equations. If
    the rafters sit at 30° to the horizontal, vertical equilibrium reads: two rafter forces ×
    sin 30° must carry 10 kN, so each rafter pushes with 10 kN in compression. Horizontal
    equilibrium then forces the tie to pull with 10 × cos 30° ≈ 8.7 kN in tension. Three
    members, one joint, three sentences of algebra — and a designer already knows which bars
    need buckling checks (the compression ones) and which need only strength (the tie).
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="put the apex working in order" widget="order" config={ceJointsOrder} />
  <p>
    Why triangles? Because a triangle is the only polygon whose shape is fixed by its side
    lengths: a rectangle of hinged bars can shear into a parallelogram, but a hinged triangle
    cannot deform without changing a bar's length. Trusses are therefore built from triangles,
    and the pattern repeats at every scale, from a roof rafter assembly to the Forth Bridge
    cantilevers. <em>Frames</em> are the generalisation: structures whose members carry forces
    at more than two points, so individual members can bend — and the analysis must cut
    through members, which is exactly where internal forces take over.
  </p>

  <h2>Centroids and the second moment of area</h2>
  <p>
    Two geometric properties decide how a cross-section behaves, and both reward physical
    understanding over memorisation. The <strong>centroid</strong> is the section's balance
    point: the point where the area would balance on a pin. Loads passed straight through it
    produce pure compression or tension; the same loads applied off-centre add bending. The
    <strong>second moment of area</strong> I — often called, loosely, the moment of inertia of
    the section — measures how far the section's area sits from the axis it bends about, each
    bit of area weighted by the <em>square</em> of its distance. Think of the merry-go-round:
    the same children are far harder to spin when they stand at the edge than at the axis,
    because distance enters squared. A hoop, with all its mass at one radius R, is the purest
    case: I = M·R². Distance, squared, is everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    The squaring is what makes I so powerful in design. For a rectangle of width b and depth
    h, <code>I = b·h³/12</code> about the horizontal axis. Take a 100 mm × 200 mm timber
    joist: I = 100 × 200³ ÷ 12 ≈ 66.7 × 10⁶ mm⁴. Now stand it the other way — 200 mm wide,
    100 mm deep — and I collapses to 16.7 × 10⁶ mm⁴, four times less. Same plank, same area,
    same weight: four times less stiffness, purely because of orientation. Doubling the depth
    again, from 200 mm to 400 mm, multiplies I by 2³ = 8. This is the whole secret of the
    I-beam and the box girder: put material as far from the bending axis as practical, and
    connect it with a thin web that mostly keeps it in place. Bending stress follows
    <code>σ = M·y ÷ I</code> — stress grows with distance y from the neutral axis (which is
    why the flanges, at maximum y, do the work) and shrinks in direct proportion to I.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="call the stiffness of the joist on edge" widget="estimate" config={ceJoistEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "more area means more stiffness." Not so — geometry
  dominates. A thin steel rule stood on edge is far harder to bend than a much heavier plank
  laid flat, and a hollow tube beats a solid rod of the same area. Area controls axial
  strength; the <em>distribution</em> of area (I) controls bending, and bending is what kills
  most beams.</p>

  <h2>Beams and the bending moment diagram</h2>
  <p>
    Cut a loaded beam at any station and equilibrium still holds for the piece on either
    side; the cut must therefore transmit an internal shear force and an internal bending
    moment. Plot that moment along the span and you have the single most informative drawing
    in structural engineering. For a simply supported beam of span L carrying a central point
    load P, statics gives a triangular diagram with peak <code>M = P·L/4</code> at mid-span:
    with P = 10 kN and L = 8 m, the peak is 10 × 8 ÷ 4 = 20 kN·m.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each diagram feature with its design use" widget="match" config={ceDiagramMatch} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="bending moment under a central point load" widget="plotter" config={trussCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the curve rises linearly from zero at each support to its
    single peak at mid-span — and the peak is the design. It tells you the maximum demand the
    worst section must carry; the zeros tell you where connections can be lighter; the shape
    tells you where to add depth or reinforcement. Distribute the same load uniformly instead
    of concentrating it and the peak drops from P·L/4 to w·L²/8 — half the value when the
    totals match. Real failure investigations often reduce to a bending moment diagram drawn
    after the fact: the load was heavier, or in a different place, than the diagram the
    designer had assumed. Every load case draws its own diagram, and the structure must
    survive the envelope of all of them.
  </p>

  <h2>Kinematics and kinetics: when loads move</h2>
  <p>
    Statics assumes the structure never accelerates. <strong>Kinematics</strong> is the
    descriptive layer: position, velocity and acceleration, and how they relate — velocity is
    the rate of change of position, acceleration the rate of change of velocity. It says
    nothing about causes. <strong>Kinetics</strong> is the causal layer: Newton's second law,
    force equals mass times acceleration, which converts an acceleration history into the
    forces the structure must resist. People have chased this connection for centuries —
    Simon Stevin is said to have dropped two balls of different mass from a church tower in
    Delft in 1586 to show they fall together, and Newton's <em>Principia</em> of 1687 turned
    such observations into laws that still describe every bridge, crane and lift built since.
  </p>
  <p>
    Civil engineers meet kinetics chiefly through moving loads, and the effects are rarely
    intuitive. A truck rolling across a bridge loads it more heavily than the same truck
    parked, because the deck must also supply the forces that deflect the vehicle's
    suspension and, in an impact, arrest its motion — design codes capture this with dynamic
    amplification factors, often around 1.3 for highway bridges. Deeper still, every structure
    has natural frequencies at which it prefers to swing, set by its stiffness and mass. When
    a load's rhythm matches a natural frequency, each cycle pumps in more energy and the
    response grows — <strong>resonance</strong>. The London Millennium Bridge delivered the
    modern object lesson: opened in June 2000, it began oscillating sideways within hours
    under synchronised lateral footfall — pedestrians unconsciously adjusting their gait to
    the deck's slight sway — forcing a closure and a two-year retrofit of dampers. The bridge
    was never under strength limits; it was a dynamics problem, and the fix was to break the
    rhythm, not to add steel.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers are small enough to work by hand. Model a timber office floor as a mass of
    2,000 kg riding on a stiffness of 3.2 MN per metre. Its natural frequency is
    <code>f = (1 ÷ 2π) × √(k ÷ m)</code> = (1 ÷ 2π) × √1,600 ≈ 6.4 Hz. Pedestrians walk at
    roughly 1.6 to 2.4 steps per second, and footfall forces carry harmonics at two and three
    times that rate, so a 6.4 Hz floor sits above the second harmonic of a brisk walk, but not
    by a comfortable margin — which is why guidance pushes floors under rhythmic occupancy
    (gyms, dance halls) above roughly 7.5 Hz: not so they are stronger, but so the crowd
    cannot find their rhythm. The same calculation, run laterally, is what the Millennium
    Bridge's designers under-weighted.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="slide to the frequency floors must clear" widget="place" config={ceFloorPlace} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> adding material always makes a structure safer. Mass is
  not free: it arrives as dead load the members must carry forever, and in an earthquake the
  inertia force is proportional to mass — a heavier building asks for a bigger earthquake
  force. The Millennium Bridge was never short of strength; its problem was mass and
  stiffness arranged so that footsteps found a rhythm. Design tunes the load path; it does
  not simply pile on steel.</p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> statics balances forces and moments on bodies that never
    accelerate; kinetics adds mass × acceleration to the same three equations, and suddenly
    trucks hit harder than their weight and crowds can walk a bridge into swaying. One
    toolkit, two regimes.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="drag the divider across the two regimes" widget="compare" config={ceRegimesCompare} />

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>A picture frame hangs motionless on a wall. State the two conditions its supports must satisfy.</li>
    <li>Why does a lighter child on a see-saw balance a heavier one by sitting farther from the pivot?</li>
    <li>A joist is laid flat instead of on edge. Its area is unchanged. What happens to its bending stiffness, and why?</li>
    <li>The Millennium Bridge was strong enough on paper. Why did it still have to close?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Net force zero in every direction <em>and</em> net moment
    zero about any point — in two dimensions, three equations. (2) The moment is force times
    lever arm; extra distance compensates for less force, keeping the moments equal. (3) It
    drops to a quarter: I = b·h³/12 swaps b and h, and depth enters cubed. Area alone never
    decides bending — distribution does. (4) Resonance: sideways footfall synchronised with a
    natural frequency of the deck, so each step pumped in energy. The problem was stiffness
    and damping, not strength.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Statics studies bodies at rest or at constant velocity; equilibrium means zero net force <em>and</em> zero net moment.</li>
    <li>Two-dimensional statics gives exactly three equations per body — a budget that decides whether a structure is determinate.</li>
    <li>Moment is force × lever arm; the see-saw balance rule and the crane's counterweight are the same idea.</li>
    <li>A truss pin joint carries only axial force, so each joint is a small equilibrium problem; triangles fix the geometry.</li>
    <li>The second moment of area weights area by distance squared — orientation can quadruple a beam's stiffness.</li>
    <li>The bending moment diagram locates the worst section; spreading the same load halves its peak.</li>
    <li>Kinematics describes motion; kinetics (F = m·a) explains it. Moving and rhythmic loads demand more than static ones.</li>
    <li>Resonance, not weakness, closed the Millennium Bridge — matching a load's rhythm to a natural frequency grows the response.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson ends where structures begin to deform. The next lesson, mechanics of
    materials, keeps the same free-body discipline but lets bodies stretch and squash:
    stress, strain and stiffness become quantities, and the internal forces you can now find
    gain allowable limits. After that, structural analysis tackles the indeterminate
    structures that statics alone could not close. Everything downstream reuses the three
    equilibrium equations you practised here.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The three-equation toolkit has a hard limit. <em>Statically indeterminate</em> structures
    — a continuous beam over several supports, a rigid frame — have more unknowns than
    equilibrium equations. Closing them requires the material's stiffness and compatibility of
    deformation: members must deflect together, and stiffer members attract more load. This is
    the gateway from second-year mechanics to real design, and it is why differential
    settlement, which redistributes load between redundant supports, matters far more than
    novices expect.
  </p>
  <p>
    Moment of inertia returns at full power in stability. A slender column under compression
    does not fail by crushing but by bowing sideways, at the Euler load
    <code>P<sub>cr</sub> = π²·E·I ÷ L²</code> — notice I appearing again, and notice the
    square: halving the effective length quarters the buckling capacity. On the dynamics side,
    the modern toolkit adds the response spectrum (for earthquakes, the peak response a
    structure of each natural period can expect), influence lines (how a load at each position
    affects one response, swept along by a moving vehicle), and serviceability limits on
    vibration even when strength is comfortable. And Newton's laws, for all their reach, are a
    model, not a monument: they lose accuracy at speeds near the speed of light, where
    Einstein's relativity takes over, and at atomic scales, where quantum effects appear.
    Within the speeds and sizes of civil engineering, though, the 1687 framework remains exact
    for every practical purpose.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a column fails when the material's crush strength is
  reached. Slender columns usually fail far earlier — by bowing sideways at the Euler load,
  which depends on stiffness E, the geometry I and the square of the effective length, and
  not at all on strength. That is why a longer column is weaker even in the same material,
  and why bracing that halves the effective length quarters the capacity: stability is a
  stiffness problem, not a strength one.</p>

